{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_13","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"find /home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/seed2094082456/7 -maxdepth 2 -type f | sort | xargs -r -I{} sh -c 'printf \\\"FILE %s\\\\n\\\" \\\"\"'$1\"; sed -n \"1,220p\" \"$1\"'\"' sh {}\"","aggregated_output":"FILE /home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/seed2094082456/7/des.md\n<new_puzzle>\n- `description`：根据边框缺口的朝向，将框内图案沿缺口方向\"倒出\"到框外，并用缺口对侧框段的颜色重新着色倒出的图案。\n- `Input structure`：背景为黑色。网格中散布若干由同一前景色绘制的单像素厚矩形边框，每个边框恰好在一条边上有一个连续缺口（缺口宽度 >= 2 像素）。每个边框内部包含若干由另一种前景色构成的小型图案像素。网格中还存在一个颜色图例区域：位于网格顶部，由若干 1x2 的色块对排成一行，每对的左格为某个边框颜色，右格为该边框\"倒出\"后图案应使用的目标颜色。\n- `Rule`：\n  1. 对每个矩形边框，找到其缺口所在的边及缺口的位置。\n  2. 将框内所有图案像素沿缺口朝向（上/下/左/右）平移，直到它们全部移出边框区域，平移距离等于从图案像素到缺口所在边的距离加 1（即刚好越过边框线）。\n  3. 在图例中查找该边框颜色对应的目标颜色，将倒出后的图案像素重新着色为该目标颜色。\n  4. 删除原边框及框内的原始图案像素，仅保留倒出后重着色的图案。\n- `Key insight`：解题需要两层独立推理——第一层是识别缺口方向决定平移方向，第二层是通过颜色图例确定输出颜色，两者缺一不可；仅观察缺口或仅观察图例都无法还原完整规则。\n</new_puzzle>\n\n<new_puzzle>\n- `description`：将散落的彩色碎片旋转并拼入灰色模板的匹配槽位中，旋转角度由碎片上的单像素标记相对于碎片重心的方位决定。\n- `Input structure`：背景为黑色。网格左侧有一个灰色（grey）单像素厚矩形轮廓构成的模板，模板内部被灰色线条分隔为若干不规则槽位（每个槽位是一个连通的背景区域）。网格右侧散布若干彩色碎片，每个碎片由一种非灰色前景色的多个像素构成一个 4-连通形状，且碎片边缘恰好有一个该碎片颜色的单独像素与碎片主体通过对角相邻但不 4-连通（即只通过 8-连通相连）。模板的每个槽位内部恰好有一个与某碎片同色的单像素标记。\n- `Rule`：\n  1. 将每个碎片与模板中同色标记的槽位配对。\n  2. 确定碎片上的 8-连通\"尾巴\"像素相对于碎片紧致包围盒中心的方位（上、右、下、左四选一）。\n  3. 确定槽位内标记像素相对于槽位紧致包围盒中心的方位。\n  4. 计算从碎片尾巴方位旋转到槽位标记方位所需的顺时针 90 度旋转次数。\n  5. 将碎片（不含尾巴像素）按该次数旋转，然后平移放入槽位中，使旋转后的碎片形状恰好填满槽位。\n  6. 输出中保留灰色模板框架，每个槽位填入对应碎片的颜色，移除右侧原始碎片。\n- `Key insight`：颜色匹配只完成配对，真正的难点在于发现 8-连通尾巴像素和槽位标记像素各自的方位差编码了旋转量——这是一种双锚点定向机制，而非简单的形状匹配。\n</new_puzzle>\n\n<new_puzzle>\n- `description`：沿每个彩色箭头指示的方向，将箭头后方的整行或整列像素\"推入\"箭头前方的封闭区域内，像素在区域边界处折叠换行。\n- `Input structure`：背景为黑色。网格中有一个由 grey 像素构成的单像素厚封闭矩形区域（内部为背景色）。矩形外侧的四条边上，各有若干紧贴边框外侧的单像素\"箭头\"标记，每个箭头标记使用不同的非灰色前景色。每个箭头标记所在的同一行（若箭头在左侧或右侧）或同一列（若箭头在上侧或下侧）上，在远离矩形的方向上排列着一串同色像素，形成一条\"待推入数据条\"。箭头在矩形左侧表示向右推入，在右侧表示向左推入，在上侧表示向下推入，在下侧表示向上推入。\n- `Rule`：\n  1. 对每个箭头标记，确定其推入方向和对应的行或列。\n  2. 收集该行/列上从箭头向外延伸的同色像素序列。\n  3. 从箭头紧邻的矩形内侧像素开始，沿推入方向逐个填充这些像素的颜色。\n  4. 若填充到达矩形对侧内壁，则折返：列方向推入时从同列矩形另一端继续，行方向推入时从同行矩形另一端继续，直到像素序列用尽。\n  5. 移除矩形外侧的所有箭头标记和数据条，保留灰色边框。\n  6. 当多个箭头的填充路径在同一像素交叉时，后处理的箭头覆盖先处理的。处理顺序为：先处理从上侧推入的箭头（按列从左到右），再处理从左侧推入的（按行从上到下），再处理从下侧推入的（按列从左到右），最后处理从右侧推入的（按行从上到下）。\n- `Key insight`：关键在于识别折叠换行机制——像素序列在矩形内部并非简单直线排列，而是蛇形折返填充；同时需要推断多条数据条的覆盖优先级。\n</new_puzzle>\n\n<new_puzzle>\n- `description`：每个前景对象根据其内部孔洞的数量，从颜色条带图例中选取对应位置的颜色进行重着色。\n- `Input structure`：背景为黑色。网格底部有一个水平颜色条带图例，由从左到右排列的若干连续色块组成（每个色块宽 1 像素、高 2 像素），颜色各不相同，用灰色单像素列分隔。网格上方散布着若干由同一种前景色（例如 blue）绘制的 4-连通封闭对象，每个对象的形状各异，内部可能包含 0 个、1 个或多个完全被该对象像素包围的背景色孔洞（孔洞为不与网格边缘相连的背景连通区域）。\n- `Rule`：\n  1. 对每个前景对象，计算其内部孔洞的数量 n（0、1、2、…）。\n  2. 在底部颜色条带图例中，从左到右第 n+1 个色块即为该对象的目标颜色（第 1 个色块对应 0 个孔洞）。\n  3. 将该对象的所有像素重着色为目标颜色，孔洞保持为背景色不变。\n  4. 输出中移除底部颜色条带图例。\n- `Key insight`：表面上看对象只是形状不同，关键洞见是必须识别拓扑属性（封闭孔洞数量）而非几何属性（大小、位置、外轮廓形状），并将其作为图例的索引键。对象外观的复杂度是干扰项，真正决定颜色的是孔洞计数。\n</new_puzzle>\n\n<new_puzzle>\n- `description`：将每个 L 形对象展开为完整矩形，展开方向由同色指示线段决定，矩形的缺失区域用对象内嵌标记的颜色填充。\n- `Input structure`：背景为黑色。网格中有若干 L 形前景对象，每个 L 形由某种颜色 A 的像素构成，形状为一个矩形切去一个矩形角落后剩余的 L 形。每个 L 形的内侧拐角处有一个颜色 B（与 A 不同）的单像素标记。此外，从每个 L 形的某条外边向外延伸出一条长度为 1 的同色 A 的线段（与 L 形主体 4-连通），这条线段位于 L 形的某条直边的中点位置。\n- `Rule`：\n  1. 对每个 L 形对象，确定其缺失角落的位置（即将 L 形补全为完整矩形时需要填充的区域）。\n  2. 找到该 L 形的外延线段所在的边——该线段指示\"展开方向\"。\n  3. 展开规则：外延线段指向的方向即为 L 形展开生长的方向。将 L 形的缺失角落区域填充为颜色 B（内嵌标记的颜色），使整体形成完整矩形。\n  4. 移除外延线段和内嵌标记像素（标记位置变为颜色 A，因为它现在是完整矩形的一部分）。\n  5. 如果外延线段指向的方向与缺失角落不在同一侧，则 L 形保持不变（不展开），仅移除线段和标记。\n- `Key insight`：L 形对象有两种可能的\"展开方向\"（向两个不同方向补全均可形成矩形），外延线段的位置消除了这一歧义；同时内嵌标记的颜色决定了补全区域的填充色，形成\"方向判定 + 颜色赋值\"的双层推理。\n</new_puzzle>\n\n<new_puzzle>\n- `description`：根据每个封闭区域内\"投票像素\"的多数颜色，将该区域的边界重着色为多数颜色，并清除区域内部的所有像素。\n- `Input structure`：背景为黑色。网格中有若干由灰色（grey）像素绘制的单像素厚封闭曲线（4-连通闭合环），这些曲线互不相交、互不嵌套，每条曲线围成一个封闭区域。每个封闭区域内部散布着若干彩色像素（称为\"投票像素\"），这些像素使用两种或三种不同的非灰非黑颜色，分布无规律。\n- `Rule`：\n  1. 对每个灰色封闭曲线围成的区域，统计区域内部各颜色投票像素的数量。\n  2. 找出数量最多的颜色（保证每个区域内存在唯一的多数颜色）。\n  3. 将该封闭曲线的所有灰色边界像素重着色为该多数颜色。\n  4. 清除区域内部的所有投票像素（变为背景色黑色）。\n  5. 灰色曲线外部的所有内容保持不变。\n- `Key insight`：灰色边框的最终颜色不是由边框自身属性决定，而是由其内部像素的\"民主投票\"结果决定；解题者需要发现内部像素的数量统计与边界重着色之间的因果关系，而非被像素的空间分布所误导。\n</new_puzzle>\n\n<new_puzzle>\n- `description`：将散落的彩色单像素种子同时向外生长，生长形状由该颜色在图例中对应的模板决定，不同种子的生长区域互相阻挡。\n- `Input structure`：背景为黑色。网格左侧有一个竖直排列的图例区域，包含若干条目，每个条目由一个 1x1 的颜色键（某前景色）和紧邻其右侧的一个小型模板图案（由同色像素在 3x3 或 4x4 范围内绘制）组成，条目之间用一行背景色分隔。网格右侧的主区域中，散布着若干单像素的\"种子\"点，每个种子的颜色与图例中某个颜色键匹配。\n- `Rule`：\n  1. 在图例中为每种颜色找到其模板图案，将模板理解为以颜色键所在位置为中心的相对偏移集合。\n  2. 对主区域中的每个种子像素，将其对应模板以种子位置为中心\"盖章\"到网格上。\n  3. 盖章时，仅覆盖背景色像素；若模板的某个偏移位置已被另一个种子的盖章或另一个种子本身占据，则跳过该像素（不覆盖）。\n  4. 盖章处理顺序：按种子在网格中从上到下、从左到右的位置逐个处理。\n  5. 输出中移除左侧图例区域，仅保留主区域及其盖章结果。\n- `Key insight`：每个种子的扩散不是简单的洪水填充，而是由图例中的模板精确定义其生长形状；难点在于发现种子之间的相互阻挡关系以及处理顺序对最终结果的影响——同一模板在不同位置会因周围种子的存在而产生不同的输出形状。\n</new_puzzle>\n\n<new_puzzle>\n- `description`：识别网格中成对的同色对象，根据两对象之间空隙的宽度选择不同的连接方式：窄间隙用实心填充连接，宽间隙用对象的轮廓线延伸连接。\n- `Input structure`：背景为黑色。网格中有若干成对出现的同色 4-连通矩形对象（每种颜色恰好出现两个矩形）。每对矩形在同一水平线或同一垂直线上对齐（即它们的行范围或列范围完全重叠）。不同颜色对之间互不干扰。\n- `Rule`：\n  1. 对每对同色矩形，测量两者之间的间隙宽度（即两矩形相对边之间的背景像素列数或行数）。\n  2. 若间隙宽度小于等于两个矩形中较小者的对齐方向边长（例如水平对齐时为较小矩形的高度），则用该颜色的实心矩形填满整个间隙区域，使两个矩形合并为一个大矩形。\n  3. 若间隙宽度大于该阈值，则仅延伸两个矩形各自面向对方的那条边的上下（或左右）端点，形成两条平行的单像素宽线段穿过间隙，将两个矩形用\"轨道\"式双线连接（线段颜色与矩形相同）。\n  4. 原始矩形保持不变。\n- `Key insight`：解题者必须发现间隙宽度与矩形自身尺寸之间的比较关系决定了连接方式的切换——这不是简单的\"连接所有同色对象\"，而是根据空间比例选择不同的连接策略。矩形尺寸同时扮演了\"被连接对象\"和\"连接方式判据\"的双重角色。\n</new_puzzle>\nFILE /home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/seed2094082456/7/ids.txt\ntrain/fc4aaf52\neval/142ca369\ntrain/2e65ae53\ntrain/66ac4c3b\ntrain/8618d23e\ntrain/42f83767\neval/dd6b8c4b\neval/7b0280bc\ntrain/d6542281\ntrain/22425bda\ntrain/97c75046\ntrain/6d1d5c90\ntrain/9f669b64\neval/36a08778\ntrain/cf5fd0ad\ntrain/230f2e48\ntrain/83b6b474\neval/e87109e9\ntrain/5ad8a7c0\ntrain/6165ea8f\nFILE /home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/seed2094082456/7/prompt.md\n你是一个 ARC-AGI 谜题设计专家。下面的 20 道参考题是难度和机制质量的标杆。仔细阅读每道题的**解题逻辑**（解题者从样例归纳规则的认知过程），而非表面场景。你的新题应在机制深度和隐蔽性上与参考题持平或者实现超越。\n\n每个参考题描述包含：\n- `description`：一句话概述题目的变换目标。\n- `Input structure`：输入网格中有哪些对象、背景、标记、边界或组件。\n- `Rule`：从输入到输出的确定性变换规则。\n- `Key insight`：解题所需的核心洞见。\n\n参考题目如下：\n<reference_puzzle index=\"1\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"fc4aaf52\" lines=\"17-34\">\n<description>\nSplit the two-color object horizontally, swap its colors, and slide the upper half to the right.\n\nInput structure:\n- The grid has one background color and exactly two foreground colors.\n- All foreground pixels form one 4-connected object with an even-height bbox.\n- The object has foreground pixels on both rows adjacent to its horizontal midpoint.\n\nRule:\n- Split the object into upper and lower halves at the midpoint of its bbox.\n- Swap the two foreground colors in both halves.\n- Keep the lower half in place and shift the upper half right until its lower-left\n  boundary pixel is diagonally adjacent to the lower half's upper-right boundary pixel.\n\nKey insight:\n- The object is not copied or reflected; it is broken at the horizontal waist,\n  recolored by exchanging the two visible object colors, then rejoined diagonally.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"2\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"142ca369\" lines=\"6-19\">\n<description>\nTrace diagonal rays from triangular arrows, reflecting off colored wall pixels.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg grid with monochrome 3-pixel L-shaped \"arrows\" (2x2 bbox missing one corner) and scattered colored wall pixels.\n\nRule:\n- Each arrow fires a ray diagonally away from its missing corner, painting bg cells in its color.\n- A head-on wall hit reverses both axes; a side-touch wall hit flips the touched axis only.\n- On any bounce the ray adopts the wall's color and recolors the contact cell.\n- Ray stops when it exits the grid or reaches another arrow.\n\nKey insight: Two distinct reflection types (corner vs side-touch) plus color inheritance at each bounce drive the path geometry.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"3\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"2e65ae53\" lines=\"88-103\">\n<description>\nFill every lattice slot from the color assigned to that slot position by the other lattices.\n\nInput structure:\n- The background is the dominant color.\n- The largest foreground color forms several one-pixel-thick rectangular lattice frames.\n- The frame lines divide each lattice into the same row-by-column set of rectangular slots; some slots already contain one non-frame color.\n\nRule:\n- Infer a color for each logical slot position from the colored slots across all lattice frames.\n- Repaint every slot interior in every lattice with the inferred color for its row/column position.\n- Keep the frame lines and all background outside frames unchanged.\n\nKey insight:\n- Slot sizes vary by frame, but the complete color pattern is indexed by lattice slot position rather than by absolute pixel size or location.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"4\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"66ac4c3b\" lines=\"57-72\">\n<description>\nComplete the sparse-color reflected copy of the foreground motif across its matching marker row or column.\n\nInput structure\n- The grid has one background color and two foreground colors.\n- One foreground color appears only as a sparse marker row or column.\n- The other foreground color contains the same sparse marker positions on a parallel row or column, plus the motif on the far side of the sparse markers.\n\nRule\n- Keep the original grid unchanged.\n- Reflect every motif pixel of the larger foreground color across the two parallel marker lines.\n- Paint the reflected motif pixels with the sparse marker color.\n\nKey insight\n- The two equal marker rows or columns are paired landmarks: the sparse-color marker line borders the source motif, and the matching source-color marker line anchors the reflected copy.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"5\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"8618d23e\" lines=\"9-25\">\n<description>\nStagger the two horizontal input regions onto a larger maroon separator canvas.\n\nInput structure:\n- The input is a rectangular grid with an even number of rows.\n- The top half and bottom half are the two regions to preserve.\n- Maroon is absent from the input and is used only as the new separator color.\n\nRule:\n- Create an output with one extra row and one extra column, filled with maroon.\n- Copy the top half into the upper-left corner.\n- Copy the bottom half one row below the top region and one column to the right.\n\nKey insight:\nThe output is not a recoloring of either region; it inserts an L-shaped maroon\ngap by diagonally offsetting the lower half from the upper half.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"6\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"42f83767\" lines=\"23-39\">\n<description>\nExpand the lower color grid by replacing each pixel with its matching top stencil.\n\nInput structure\nThe input has a top legend and a lower dense color grid, separated by blank rows.\nThe legend contains left-to-right 2x2 color swatches and, to their right,\nthe same number of square neutral-color stencil masks.\n\nRule\nPair the swatches and stencil masks by left-to-right order. For every pixel in\nthe lower color grid, paste the paired stencil mask as one output block,\nrecoloring the mask pixels with that pixel's color and leaving mask holes as bg.\n\nKey insight\nThe lower grid is not copied directly; it is an index grid whose colors select\nthe stencil shape from the legend, so each input pixel becomes one square block.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"7\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"dd6b8c4b\" lines=\"9-22\">\n<description>\nMove the closest reachable maroon pixels into the 3×3 block around the unique red center.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = orange floor, magenta walls, scattered maroon (9) pixels.\n- A unique red (2) pixel marks the center of a 3×3 destination block.\n\nRule:\n- Maroon pixels connected to the destination through floor/maroon cells (not blocked by walls) are candidates.\n- Rank candidates by distance to the destination; the closest ones fill the block in row-major order.\n- Moved maroons are erased (replaced with floor color).\n\nKey insight: Wall barriers restrict connectivity — only maroon pixels reachable via flood-fill through the floor qualify, so proximity alone isn't enough.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"8\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"7b0280bc\" lines=\"54-69\">\n<description>\nRecolor the shortest path between two terminal squares in a square-and-track graph.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = most common color.\n- Two non-bg colors form solid NxN squares (N>=2): one color marks exactly 2 terminals, the other marks stations.\n- A third non-bg color forms 1-cell-wide track segments connecting pairs of squares (graph edges).\n\nRule:\n- Find the shortest path (fewest edges) between the two terminal squares.\n- Recolor every station square on that path to green.\n- Recolor every track segment along that path to grey.\n- Everything else unchanged.\n\nKey insight: The grid encodes a graph where squares are nodes and tracks are edges; the task is graph shortest-path, not spatial proximity.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"9\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"d6542281\" lines=\"72-91\">\n<description>\nComplete each partial marker mask by stamping the matching multicolor sprite over it.\n\nInput structure\n- A dominant background contains one or more 8-connected multicolor sprites.\n- Separate target masks use colors that already occur inside one of those sprites.\n- A target mask may contain only one marker pixel or several same-alignment marker pixels.\n\nRule\n- For each multicolor sprite, test every translation that aligns a target pixel with a\n  same-color sprite pixel.\n- Keep the translations with maximal exact overlap against the existing target pixels,\n  rejecting translations that conflict with other foreground or overlap a source sprite.\n- Stamp the whole sprite at those translations; if a non-marker sprite color occurs as\n  a single source pixel, consume that source-only marker back to background.\n\nKey insight\nThe scattered target pixels are partial copies: their same-color overlap with a sprite\ndetermines where the full sprite must be completed.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"10\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"22425bda\" lines=\"58-76\">\n<description>\nList the colors of overlaid straight lines in their only possible bottom-to-top crossing order.\n\nInput structure\nThe background is the dominant color. Every foreground color is the visible part\nof one full one-cell-thick horizontal, vertical, or 45-degree diagonal line.\nHigher lines may hide pixels of lower lines at crossings.\n\nRule\nRecover each full line from its visible collinear pixels. At any cell where\nmultiple recovered lines cross, the visible color is above every other line\nthrough that cell. Return the unique ordering of all line colors that respects\nthose above/below constraints and whose consecutive colors are lines that\ngeometrically cross.\n\nKey insight\nThe visible crossing colors give a partial layer order, and the intended answer\nis the unique Hamiltonian chain through the line-intersection graph from the\nbottom layer to the top layer.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"11\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"97c75046\" lines=\"88-107\">\n<description>\nMove the single marker to the endpoint reached by sliding along the obstacle contour.\n\nInput structure:\n- A dominant background color fills the canvas.\n- One single-pixel marker sits away from a connected obstacle region of the\n  remaining color.\n- The marker has exactly one horizontal or vertical sightline to the obstacle.\n\nRule:\n- Find the last background pixel before the obstacle along that sightline.\n- From that pixel, try the two diagonal turns that continue around the\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
